39:- **B2.4** An **active** entry must also have: a `kind` from the five (decision, fact, lesson, observation,
41:- **B2.5** A problem in an inactive entry beyond B2.3 is a warning, not an error, so one old entry cannot block every
43:- **B2.6** A file that is not valid UTF-8 is reported as `<file>: <problem>`, not as a crash.
44:- **Fails if** a malformed active entry gets a line, a prefix/kind mismatch passes, a duplicate id passes, a broken
48:- **B3.1** One line per active entry, sorted by file name in code-point order:
50:- **B3.2** A fixed head: the title `# Project memory index`, then a comment saying the file is generated, must not be
52:- **B3.3** UTF-8, LF line endings, one newline at the end. No date, time, count, host name or locale-dependent order.
53:- **B3.4** If the result is byte-identical to the existing file, the file is not rewritten. A missing `INDEX.md` is
55:- **Fails if** two runs over the same entries, on two machines or with two locales, give different bytes; a
59:- **B4.1** The index must fit the strictest start-file limit of the agents that read it (Rd3.1). The script measures
63:- **B4.2** Every run prints the size against both limits.
64:- **B4.3** Nothing is ever cut to fit. Over the limit, write mode still writes the whole file, prints a warning and
66:- **B4.4** Any change that brings the index back under the limit makes the check pass. No approval is needed (d W4.1).
67:- **Open:** which agents and load paths read `INDEX.md`, and whether any of them counts characters rather than bytes
69:- **Fails if** a line is dropped or shortened to fit, or `--check` passes an index over either limit.
71:## C — Check: how anyone tells the file is right
74:- **C1.1** `python3 tools/memory_index.py --check [<rev>]` rebuilds the index from the snapshot (B1.3) and compares it
76:- **C1.2** It passes (exit 0) only when the entries are valid, the bytes are equal, and the size is within the limits.
79:- **C1.3** A snapshot with no `project-memory/`, or an unborn `HEAD`, passes with "nothing to check".
80:- **C1.4** It writes nothing.
81:- **Fails if** a hand edit to `INDEX.md` passes, a stale `INDEX.md` passes, or check mode changes any file.
84:- **C2.1** `tests/test_memory_index.py` runs under the repo's `pytest`
86:- **C2.2** It holds at least these tests:
87:  - golden bytes for a fixed set of sample entries;
88:  - running twice changes nothing;
89:  - the check rejects a hand-edited `INDEX.md`;
90:  - the check rejects a superseded entry listed as active;
91:  - `--check HEAD` passes on this repo itself.
92:- **Fails if** any of these cases passes when it should fail, or the test file does not run under the repo's `pytest`.
94:## G — Git: when it runs, and how branches meet
97:- **G1.1** Whoever creates or changes any entry file runs the script, of every kind and for every status change, and
99:- **G1.2** This is written as an instruction where agents read it: `CLAUDE.md` today, because `AGENTS.md` only points
101:- **Fails if** a commit changes an entry and not the `INDEX.md` it implies (caught by C1 at the next check).
104:- **G2.1** One committed hook, `.githooks/pre-commit`, runs `--check` when the staged change touches
106:- **G2.2** It is switched on once per clone with `git config core.hooksPath .githooks`.
107:- **G2.3** The hook is a convenience, not the guarantee. Rebase, cherry-pick, fast-forward and `--no-verify` skip it;
109:- **Fails if** a commit through the hook leaves a wrong `INDEX.md`, or a change to the script alone skips the check.
112:- **G3.1** `INDEX.md` merges as normal text. There is no `merge=union`, no `merge=binary` and no custom merge driver.
113:- **G3.2** When `INDEX.md` conflicts, nobody edits it. Resolve the entry files first, then run the script, stage
115:- **Fails if** a merged `INDEX.md` differs from what the script builds from the merged entries.
118:- **G4.1** An entry's `id` is unique without coordination between branches. It is never "the next free number". The
120:- **G4.2** The script rejects a duplicate id (B2.3); it does not choose the form.
121:- **Fails if** two branches can each create the same id and the clash shows only after a merge.
